# Task 2.2 - showing a message only when it makes sense.
#
# Task 2.1 gave every process a vector clock, which is enough to say what is
# ordered and what is not. It is not enough on its own: knowing that B depends
# on A does not stop B being displayed first if B gets there first.
#
# This is a chat. Every line goes to everyone — that is what `broadcast` is,
# and the rule below is only defined for messages that go to everyone. "The
# next message p2 sent" means nothing if p2 sends different things to
# different people.
#
# `late=p3` says one copy takes the slow route. Everyone else has it at once;
# p3 does not. That is the only unusual thing in the program, and it is what
# makes the problem visible at all.
#
# The rule, once delivery is a decision, is:
#
#     a message from p_j carrying stamp V is delivered at p_i only when
#         V[j] == V_i[j] + 1        it is the very next thing p_j sent
#         V[k] <= V_i[k]  for k≠j   and I have seen everything p_j had seen
#
# A message that fails it is held, not dropped, and offered again every time
# something is delivered — because a delivery is exactly the event that can
# release it.
#
# Try these in order. Steps 1 and 2 are the comparison the task is about.
#
#   1. run it as it is, with the rule switched off. Read p3's line. It is
#      shown the reply before the message being replied to, and there is
#      nothing in the vector clock stopping that
#   2. switch the rule on, and read p3's line again. Note which message is
#      held, what released it, and that no message was lost
#   3. make p1 slow. The order does not change. Delivery order is about the
#      messages, not about who is fast
#   4. add a third message and choose who is late, then predict what p3 sees
#      before you run it
#   5. one to answer, not to edit: every process now displays the same
#      order. Is that the same thing as every process displaying it at the
#      same time, and does the chat need the second?

# --- the machines ----------------------------------------------------

@process
class Node:
    pass

p1 = Node(speed=1.0)      # step 3: make it 0.3
p2 = Node(speed=1.0)
p3 = Node(speed=1.0)

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[p1, p2, p3])

# --- the conversation ------------------------------------------------

def story() -> void:
    # p1 says something, and p3's copy is slow to arrive
    p1.broadcast("A", late=p3)
    # p2 has seen A, and answers it. Everyone gets this one straight away,
    # p3 included — so p3 has the answer before the question
    p2.broadcast("B")
    # step 4: add a third line here, and pick who is late

# step 2: add   , delivery="causal"
job = Calls(run=story)

world.run(job)
